# Default for every path: git decides text vs binary, stores text as LF, and checks
# it out native. This line makes the result the same on every machine whatever its
# own core.autocrlf says - the repository is worked on from both Windows and macOS,
# and without it a Windows clone with autocrlf=false commits CRLF that a macOS
# clone then shows as modified.
* text=auto

# Line endings are LOAD-BEARING for the two launcher scripts, in opposite directions.
#
# run.sh  Its shebang is #!/bin/sh. Ending that line with CR makes a Unix
#         kernel look for an interpreter literally named "sh" + CR. It reports
#         "bad interpreter: no such file or directory" - naming a shell that
#         plainly does exist, so it reads as the plugin being broken.
# run.cmd cmd.exe mis-parses a .cmd file saved with bare LF: measured here, a
#         label or a multi-line block silently does the wrong thing.
#
# Without this file, git on Windows normalises both to CRLF on checkout and the
# .sh breaks for everyone who clones on Linux or macOS.
*.sh  text eol=lf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf

# Never converted, whatever the content looks like.
*.png binary
*.exe binary
